18:["$","div",null,{"className":"_heroFrame_jyrx9_343","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"_heroCopy_jyrx9_354","children":[["$","h1",null,{"id":"opening-title","className":"_title_jyrx9_360","children":"When the Show Ends"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"_subtitle_jyrx9_371","children":"Captivity, care and the responsibilities that outlast entertainment"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"_lead_jyrx9_381","children":[["$","$1c","text-0",{"children":"An aquarium encounter has a beginning and an ending for the visitor. Someone approaches the glass, watches a beluga move through the water, perhaps takes a photograph, and eventually leaves. The animal's life does not end with that encounter. Its environment remains organised by the institution through which it is being seen."}]]}],["$","a",null,{"className":"_beginLink_jyrx9_397","href":"#introduction-body","children":"Begin reading"}]]}]}]
19:["$","p",null,{"className":"_caption_jyrx9_423","children":"Conceptual ocean imagery; not documentary footage of Marineland"}]
1a:["$","p",null,{"id":"opening-description","className":"_srOnly_jyrx9_438","children":"As the page scrolls, the camera moves from a silver-blue dusk ocean surface, through a refracting waterline where a beluga passes, and into a quiet deep blue underwater environment."}]
1b:[[["$","link","css:/when-the-show-ends/assets/index-ClHGeSOg.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/when-the-show-ends/assets/index-ClHGeSOg.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/when-the-show-ends/assets/index-ClHGeSOg.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"id":"introduction-body","className":"_introductionBody_166zd_1","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"_readingColumn_166zd_11","children":[[["$","p","This website begins with that difference in duration and decision-making. It asks what happens when a marine attraction closes, but the animals associated with it still require a place to live and an arrangement capable of supporting their needs. The central issue is not only whether captivity should continue. It is also how humans should respond to the lives that previous decisions have already shaped.",{"children":[["$","$1c","text-0",{"children":"This website begins with that difference in duration and decision-making. It asks what happens when a marine attraction closes, but the animals associated with it still require a place to live and an arrangement capable of supporting their needs. The central issue is not only whether captivity should continue. It is also how humans should respond to the lives that previous decisions have already shaped."}]]}],["$","p","Using Marineland Canada as a case study, the following chapters connect animal welfare, opportunities for agency and the ethics of continuing responsibility. The discussion moves from the visitor's encounter to the animal's living conditions, then to the practical and ethical consequences of institutional closure.",{"children":[["$","$1c","text-0",{"children":"Using Marineland Canada as a case study, the following chapters connect animal welfare, opportunities for agency and the ethics of continuing responsibility. The discussion moves from the visitor's encounter to the animal's living conditions, then to the practical and ethical consequences of institutional closure."}]]}],["$","p","The argument developed here is that ending a system does not automatically resolve obligations arising within it. An appropriate response must consider both immediate protection and the quality of the life that follows. Keeping an animal alive, deciding where it will live and providing opportunities for a worthwhile life are related questions, but they are not identical.",{"children":[["$","$1c","text-0",{"children":"The argument developed here is that ending a system does not automatically resolve obligations arising within it. An appropriate response must consider both immediate protection and the quality of the life that follows. Keeping an animal alive, deciding where it will live and providing opportunities for a worthwhile life are related questions, but they are not identical."}]]}]],["$","p",null,{"className":"_argumentQuestion_166zd_12","children":"When the show ends, who remains responsible?"}]]}]}],["$","$L1d",null,{"chapter":{"id":"encounter","number":"01","eyebrow":"The Encounter","title":"One encounter. Two realities.","lead":["An aquarium can make an unfamiliar animal visible at close range. For a visitor, that proximity may be fascinating, educational or emotionally significant. Yet seeing an animal clearly is not the same as understanding the conditions of its life. The frame of the encounter directs attention towards what can be watched, while the arrangements that sustain the animal may remain less visible.","This website treats the glass as a visual metaphor for that distinction. It offers access to the observer while also marking a boundary within a managed environment. The same scene can therefore be approached through two different questions: what does the visitor receive from the encounter, and what conditions organise the animal's continuing life?"],"sections":[{"id":"visitor-view","heading":"Visitor View","paragraphs":["From the visitor's position, arrival, attention and departure are largely matters of personal choice. A visit occupies a limited period within a much larger life. The observer can move away, redirect attention or decide not to participate. The experience may remain meaningful after departure, but the visitor is not required to remain in the setting that produced it."]},{"id":"beluga-view","heading":"Beluga View","paragraphs":["From the perspective of management conditions, a different set of questions becomes important. Who determines access to space? How are feeding and medical routines arranged? How are opportunities for interaction or separation provided? Who decides whether the animal will be moved?","These questions do not claim to reproduce a beluga's thoughts. They redirect attention from the visitor's experience to the conditions that humans arrange around the animal. The objective is to examine differences in control without inventing an animal's internal monologue."]},{"id":"attentive-empathy","heading":"Theoretical perspective: attentive empathy","paragraphs":["Lori Gruen's account of entangled empathy asks people to respond to animals through attention to their particular needs, vulnerabilities and relationships, rather than treating ethical concern as a detached response to an abstract category. [R6]","Applied here, this encourages a more careful form of looking. Concern should lead to questions about evidence and individual circumstances, not to confidence that a beautiful image reveals happiness or that a dark image proves suffering. The website therefore uses visual comparison to initiate inquiry, rather than to claim direct access to another species' experience."]}],"closing":"Your visit ends. Their life in human care continues."}}],"$L1e","$L1f","$L20","$L21","$L22"]]
23:I["bf5967b85b1b",[],"HumanCareChapter",1]
24:I["be0253760fce",[],"MarinelandChapter",1]
30:I["f24bc05cee90",[],"ResponsibilityChapter",1]
31:I["ce097b0baa17",[],"SourcesSection",1]
1e:["$","$L23",null,{"chapter":{"id":"human-care","number":"02","eyebrow":"A Life in Human Care","title":"More than survival","lead":["Belugas inhabit Arctic and sub-Arctic waters and are social animals that form groups for activities including hunting, migration and interaction. Their lives also depend on sensory capacities that are not captured by an image of a whale swimming past glass. NOAA identifies sound as important to their communication and echolocation. [R1]","These characteristics provide a starting point for asking what a suitable environment should support. They do not establish that every individual has identical needs, or that a visually natural environment is automatically appropriate. The relevant question is how particular conditions affect a particular animal over time."],"sections":[{"id":"animal-welfare","heading":"2.1 Animal welfare: what does a life feel like?","paragraphs":["The Five Domains Model separates four areas of evidence: nutrition, physical environment, health and behavioural interactions. A fifth domain concerns mental state. The first four help inform cautious assessments of the animal's experiences; the fifth is not simply another resource that can be supplied. [R3]","The framework is useful here because it directs attention beyond whether an animal survives. It also considers potentially positive experiences and the effects of interactions with surroundings, other animals and people. A pool, a feeding schedule or a veterinary service matters through what it enables or prevents in an individual's life. [R3]","For this website, the framework becomes a set of questions rather than a diagnostic instrument. What conditions are available? What does the animal actually do? What changes over time? What evidence supports an interpretation, and what remains uncertain? A website visitor cannot establish an animal's welfare from a photograph or a short clip."]},{"id":"movement-agency","heading":"2.2 Movement and agency: the opportunity to act","paragraphs":["Space can be considered as more than a quantity. An assessment can also ask how an animal uses an environment, whether it can move between different conditions, and whether meaningful alternatives are available. A larger area does not, by itself, answer every question about the opportunities it provides.","Littlewood, Heslop and Cobb discuss animal agency through qualities including choice, control and challenge. Their framework considers animals as active participants in their lives, not only recipients of resources. It also distinguishes an opportunity being offered from an animal being able to use it. [R4]","Applied to this project, an agency-focused question might be whether an animal can approach an interaction, move away from it, explore a different area or engage in an appropriate activity. These are questions for investigation, not claims that a particular facility necessarily provides or denies each opportunity.","Crucially, receiving care and exercising agency need not be opposites. The ethical challenge is to consider how necessary support could coexist with meaningful opportunities to act, rather than assuming that dependency removes the importance of choice. [R4]"]},{"id":"sound","heading":"2.3 Sound: understanding a sensory world","paragraphs":["Belugas produce several kinds of sounds, including whistles, calls and echolocation clicks. NOAA describes echolocation as a way of obtaining information from returning sound, helping belugas detect objects and locate prey. Listening to a recording can therefore draw attention to an aspect of their lives that a visual display alone may overlook. [R2]","The recording presented here is an invitation to listen carefully, not an invitation to translate every sound into an emotion. A call should not be labelled as happy, lonely or distressed unless the particular interpretation is supported by the recording's documentation. Its meaning cannot be inferred from how it sounds to a human listener.","There are also limits to this encounter. A recording played through a phone or laptop does not recreate a beluga's auditory experience. Beluga echolocation can include ultrasonic frequencies beyond ordinary human hearing, and any processing used to make a sample accessible should be explained. [R17]","The ethical value of this interaction is therefore not that it allows us to become a whale. It makes our own limits more apparent and encourages attention to an animal whose ways of sensing differ from ours."],"closing":"Listen to a recording. Then consider what listening cannot tell us."},{"id":"individual-histories","heading":"2.4 Food, health and individual histories","paragraphs":["An animal's future cannot be decided solely by identifying its species. It is also necessary to consider its history and current condition. NOAA's rehabilitation guidance explains that developmental, behavioural, ecological and medical factors can influence whether a rescued marine mammal is considered releasable. This guidance concerns rehabilitation cases; it does not provide a ready-made verdict for Marineland's whales. [R13]","For the present discussion, its significance is methodological: a proposed outcome needs evidence about the individual. Being capable of swimming is not the same as demonstrating that all requirements for independent life have been met. Equally, a history of human support should not be used as an automatic reason to dismiss every possibility of change.","A responsible assessment would ask what abilities are established, what support remains necessary, what preparation could be appropriate and which uncertainties cannot yet be resolved. The aim is neither to romanticise release nor to treat existing arrangements as beyond question."]},{"id":"relationships","heading":"2.5 Relationships as part of care","paragraphs":["Social life also complicates the idea that relocation is simply a change of address. In its July 2026 announcement, Shedd Aquarium described keeping four arriving female belugas together because of their existing relationships. This is a receiving institution's account of a particular decision, not evidence that all belugas require the same group arrangement. [R9]","The case prompts a broader question: what should travel with an animal besides its body? Medical records, knowledge of familiar cues and information about social relationships may all be relevant subjects for a care handover. A destination should be considered not only as a physical space but as a setting in which relationships and routines will continue or change."],"closing":"A future placement concerns a life with a history, not an interchangeable member of a species."}]}}]
1f:["$","$L24",null,{"chapter":{"id":"marineland","number":"03","eyebrow":"When the Doors Close","title":"Marineland Canada: a case of continuing obligations","lead":["The Marineland case connects the preceding ideas to a sequence of institutional decisions. It shows why a change in law, an export decision, a transport operation and an animal's subsequent welfare should not be treated as the same event. Each concerns a different stage of responsibility.","The following timeline presents selected records, not a complete institutional history. Government documents describe legal and administrative decisions. Receiving aquariums describe their participation and subsequent care. These sources are useful, but their roles and limits must remain visible."],"sections":[],"closing":"Arrival is a milestone. Care continues."}}]
20:[[["$","link","css:/when-the-show-ends/assets/index-ClHGeSOg.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/when-the-show-ends/assets/index-ClHGeSOg.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/when-the-show-ends/assets/index-ClHGeSOg.css"}],"$undefined"],["$","section",null,{"id":"dilemma","className":"_chapter_zyl3o_1","data-chapter":"dilemma","aria-labelledby":"dilemma-title","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"_inner_zyl3o_13","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"_chapterHeading_zyl3o_18","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"_chapterNumber_zyl3o_28","aria-hidden":"true","children":"04"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"_eyebrow_zyl3o_29","children":"The Dilemma"}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"dilemma-title","children":"Evaluating conditions, not choosing a slogan"}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"_lead_zyl3o_82","children":[["$","p","dilemma-lead-0",{"children":[["$","$1c","text-0",{"children":"Four pathways frequently frame this discussion: release, relocation, sanctuary care and remaining at the existing facility. Here they are analytical categories for comparing responsibilities, not a claim that all four were immediately available to every Marineland animal."}]]}],["$","p","dilemma-lead-1",{"children":[["$","$1c","text-0",{"children":"An ethical comparison must consider time as well as place. An arrangement that might be desirable after preparation may not be ready during an immediate care crisis. Conversely, a necessary short-term decision should not automatically be treated as the best permanent outcome."}]]}],["$","p","dilemma-lead-2",{"children":[["$","$1c","text-0",{"children":"The following questions are this project's framework for discussion, not veterinary instructions or a complete regulatory checklist. Each pathway should be examined through possible benefits, necessary conditions, continuing support and unresolved concerns."}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"_pathwaySequence_zyl3o_126","children":[["$","article","release",{"className":"_pathway_zyl3o_30","data-pathway":"release","aria-labelledby":"pathway-release-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"_pathwayNumber_zyl3o_30","aria-hidden":"true","children":"4.1"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"_pathwayBody_zyl3o_145","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"_pathwayHeading_zyl3o_149","children":[["$","h3",null,{"id":"pathway-release-title","children":"Release"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Could independent life become possible?"}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"_pathwayIllustration_zyl3o_178","data-pathway-illustration":"release","data-media-id":"pathway-release-illustration","children":[["$","ol",null,{"role":"img","aria-label":"Three related conditions: current support, individual assessment and possible independence.","children":[["$","li","Current support",{"children":["$","strong",null,{"children":"Current support"}]}],["$","li","Individual assessment",{"children":["$","strong",null,{"children":"Individual assessment"}]}],["$","li","Possible independence",{"children":["$","strong",null,{"children":"Possible independence"}]}]]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":"Conceptual relationship between conditions; not a documented plan for an individual animal."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"_pathwayProse_zyl3o_95","children":[["$","p","release-0",{"children":[["$","$1c","text-0",{"children":"Release expresses a powerful aspiration: that an animal might live without permanent institutional confinement. However, an aspiration is not the same as evidence that a particular release would be appropriate."}]]}],["$","p","release-1",{"children":[["$","$1c","text-0",{"children":"NOAA's rehabilitation guidance considers the animal's condition and behaviour, as well as possible effects on wild populations and post-release identification or monitoring. These principles show why suitability must be assessed rather than presumed. Their application to a long-term captive individual would require relevant expertise and case-specific evidence. "}],"$L25"]}],"$L26"]}],"$L27","$L28","$L29"]}]]}],"$L2a","$L2b","$L2c"]}],["$L2d"],"$L2e","$L2f"]}]}]]
21:["$","$L30",null,{"chapter":{"id":"responsibility","number":"05","eyebrow":"Responsibility Remains","title":"What does it mean to remain responsible?","lead":["The moment of closure is visible. The work of continuing care is less easily contained in a single image. It includes repeated decisions, changing needs and commitments that extend beyond the moment when an institution stops attracting visitors.","The ethical question is therefore not only who can receive an animal, but who should remain answerable for the life that follows. An account of responsibility needs to consider the history that produced the situation, the capacities required to respond and the ways in which promises can be examined."],"sections":[{"id":"relational-responsibility","heading":"Relational responsibility: why history matters","paragraphs":["In *Animal Ethics in Context*, Clare Palmer argues that obligations to assist animals can depend on relationships, histories and human involvement in producing dependency or vulnerability. On this account, similar capacities do not necessarily mean that every animal stands in an identical relationship to human duties of assistance. [R5]","Applied to the present project, this perspective supports a particular argument: when human decisions have shaped the conditions on which an animal relies, withdrawing from those conditions requires an account of what will replace them. The argument is not that all forms of captivity must therefore continue. It is that ending or changing a system should include responsibility for the animals affected by the transition.","Dependency is not used here as a reason to dismiss freedom or agency. It identifies a relationship that must be taken seriously while alternatives are considered. A responsible future should ask both what support remains necessary and how unnecessary restrictions could be reduced."]},{"id":"accountability","heading":"Accountability: turning concern into commitments","paragraphs":["This website proposes that a care commitment should be examinable. It should identify who provides support, how that support is sustained, what is being observed and what will happen when circumstances change. Good intentions matter, but they do not substitute for an arrangement that can continue operating.","The original operator, a receiving organisation, regulatory bodies and relevant specialists may occupy different roles. Their responsibilities should not be treated as interchangeable, and ethical expectations should not be presented as established legal duties without jurisdiction-specific evidence.","The public's role is also different from that of a veterinarian or a regulator. Public attention can demand clear information and resist premature narratives of closure. It cannot replace individual assessment with a vote on which destination appears most appealing."]},{"id":"open-questions","heading":"Questions that should remain open","paragraphs":["These questions turn the ending of a show into the beginning of sustained scrutiny. They also resist two misleading conclusions: that moving an animal automatically resolves the problem, or that an imperfect choice makes continued responsibility pointless.","A responsible decision may remain difficult. It should nevertheless be open to evidence, explanation and revision."]},{"id":"closing-statement","heading":"Closing statement","paragraphs":["An institution can stop selling tickets. An exhibition can end. A transfer can be completed. None of these moments, by itself, settles what is owed to the animals whose lives have been shaped by the institution.","*When the Show Ends* asks the reader to understand closure as a transition in responsibility rather than its disappearance. The measure of that transition is not simply whether an animal has left one place, but whether its continuing life is being supported in ways that can be justified and sustained."],"closing":"The doors can close in a day. Dependency does not."}],"closing":"When the show ends, who remains responsible?"}}]
22:["$","$L31",null,{"chapter":{"id":"sources","number":null,"eyebrow":"Sources","title":"Sources, Method and Media Credits","lead":["This website combines scientific literature, ethical scholarship, government records and statements from organisations involved in animal care or sanctuary development. These sources answer different questions. A theoretical framework helps structure an argument; it does not establish the facts of an individual case. An institutional update documents what its publisher reports; it is not automatically an independent welfare assessment.","The article distinguishes reported events from interpretation and retains uncertainty where the available evidence does not resolve a question. The Marineland material presents selected records rather than a complete history or a current census of animals.","Conceptual imagery is used to explain relationships and questions. It should not be mistaken for documentary evidence. Recordings retain their own provenance and do not represent the sounds of Marineland unless the documentation explicitly identifies that location."],"sections":[],"closing":"Evidence reviewed: 9 October 2026."}}]
